From 99f53e8f7ad77a5be374fdfa019f972c4f73b1e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 18:20:32 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] usb: device: do not re-init transfer slots on every
 usb_enable()

usb_enable() calls usb_transfer_init() unconditionally, and that ran
k_work_init() + k_sem_init() over every transfer slot. k_work_init()
overwrites the whole k_work, list node included, and a work item can
still be linked in the workqueue's pending list at that point:
usb_disable() submits the cancel completions and nothing drains them
before usb_enable() runs again.

Zeroing a linked node and appending it later sets node->next = NULL
mid-list, truncating the pending list and orphaning every item behind
it. Orphaned items keep K_WORK_QUEUED_BIT set while being unreachable
from the list head, so the queue can never run them and every later
k_work_submit() for them is a no-op ('already queued'). Their transfers
never complete, their slots never return to the pool, and
usb_ep_get_transfer() keeps matching them, so those endpoints stay
blocked until reboot. Re-initializing the semaphores is unsound for the
same reason: a slot still in use is handed to a second owner.

Symptom on hardware (Telink B91, CDC-ACM + HID composite): after USB
re-attach churn the CDC ACM console goes dead in both directions while
HID keeps working and the host still sees a healthy configured device;
the state survives physical re-enumeration and only a power cycle clears
it. Captured with an out-of-band watchdog: the stranded work items were
flagged queued, pointed at the live USB workqueue, and were absent from
its pending list.

Initialize the slots once. Verified by 20 usb_disable()/usb_enable()
cycles racing live SMP traffic, which reproduced the wedge 100% of the
time before this change and 0/20 after.

Signed-off-by: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
---
 subsys/usb/device/usb_transfer.c | 29 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/subsys/usb/device/usb_transfer.c b/subsys/usb/device/usb_transfer.c
index 0e24995d2..1013c01e9 100644
--- a/subsys/usb/device/usb_transfer.c
+++ b/subsys/usb/device/usb_transfer.c
@@ -435,13 +435,40 @@ int usb_transfer_sync(uint8_t ep, uint8_t *data, size_t dlen, unsigned int flags
 	return pdata.tsize;
 }
 
-/* Init transfer slots */
+/* Init transfer slots.
+ *
+ * usb_enable() calls this on every enable, but the slots' kernel objects must
+ * only ever be initialized ONCE.  k_work_init() overwrites the whole k_work,
+ * including its list node, and a work item can still be linked in the
+ * workqueue's pending list here: usb_disable() submits the cancel completions
+ * and nothing drains them before usb_enable() runs again.  Zeroing a linked
+ * node, then appending it later, sets node->next = NULL mid-list — truncating
+ * the pending list and orphaning every item behind it.  Those items keep
+ * K_WORK_QUEUED_BIT set while being unreachable from the list head, so the
+ * queue can never run them and every later k_work_submit() for them is a
+ * no-op: their transfers never complete, their slots never return to the pool
+ * and their endpoints stay blocked until reboot.  Re-initializing the
+ * semaphores is equally unsound — it can hand a slot that is still in use to
+ * a second owner.
+ *
+ * Observed on hardware as a CDC ACM console dead in both directions (HID
+ * unaffected) after USB re-attach churn, surviving re-enumeration, with the
+ * stranded work items flagged queued but absent from the queue's list.
+ */
 int usb_transfer_init(void)
 {
+	static bool initialized;
+
+	if (initialized) {
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	for (size_t i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(ut_data); i++) {
 		k_work_init(&ut_data[i].work, usb_transfer_work);
 		k_sem_init(&ut_data[i].sem, 1, 1);
 	}
 
+	initialized = true;
+
 	return 0;
 }
